Bluetooth: survive a proto ->create() that succeeds but leaves sk NULL.

Opening ANY AF_BLUETOOTH socket panics this kernel. bluetoothd does it within
a minute of starting, so enabling the bluetooth service turns the device into
a ~90-second reboot loop that presents as "the tablet keeps turning itself
off". Diagnosed 2026-08-02 from two independent ramoops captures:

    Internal error: Oops - BUG: 0 [#1] PREEMPT SMP
    CPU: 3 PID: 3349 Comm: bluetoothd Tainted: G S WC 5.4.254-qgki #7
    pc : bt_sock_reclassify_lock+0x5c/0x60
    lr : bt_sock_create+0xdc/0x108
    x0 : 0000000000000000
    Call trace:
     bt_sock_reclassify_lock+0x5c/0x60
     __sock_create+0x164/0x2dc
     __sys_socket+0x60/0x120

x0 is the sk argument: NULL. bt_sock_create() does

    err = bt_proto[proto]->create(net, sock, proto, kern);
    if (!err)
        bt_sock_reclassify_lock(sock->sk, proto);

and bt_sock_reclassify_lock() opens with BUG_ON(!sk). Some proto backend in
this downstream tree (proto 1, BTPROTO_HCI, per x1/x2/x20 in the dump)
returns 0 with sock->sk never populated, so the BUG_ON fires and Samsung's
sec_debug escalates the Oops to a full panic + watchdog bite.

The backend lying about success is the real bug, but it is vendor blob-side
glue; guarding the core costs one branch and turns "any BT socket = reboot"
into "BT socket fails with -EPROTO", which userspace (bluetoothd) already
handles by giving up cleanly.

Upstream kernels never see this because their hci_sock_create always sets sk
or returns an error; do NOT try to upstream this guard, fix the caller in
techpack instead if BT is ever actually wanted.

--- a/net/bluetooth/af_bluetooth.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/af_bluetooth.c
@@ -129,6 +129,17 @@
 	if (bt_proto[proto] && try_module_get(bt_proto[proto]->owner)) {
 		err = bt_proto[proto]->create(net, sock, proto, kern);
-		if (!err)
+		if (!err && !sock->sk) {
+			/*
+			 * Downstream HCI glue can return success while never
+			 * attaching an sk. bt_sock_reclassify_lock() BUG_ONs
+			 * on that, which on this device is a full panic plus
+			 * watchdog reboot. Refuse the socket instead.
+			 */
+			WARN_ONCE(1, "BT proto %d create() left sk NULL\n",
+				  proto);
+			err = -EPROTO;
+		}
+		if (!err)
 			bt_sock_reclassify_lock(sock->sk, proto);
 		module_put(bt_proto[proto]->owner);
 	}
